# Authentication (Better Auth)

The starter ships local email/password sign-in. It is not a complete
authentication system: registration is a CLI command, and password reset,
email verification, OAuth, and roles are deliberately deferred (see below).
Two defaults are already safe: `/admin` admits only the accounts listed in
`ADMIN_EMAILS`, and `POST /api/login` is throttled per email and client
address by the `auth` plugin's default limiter.

## One-time setup

1. Start MariaDB. Auth needs a running MariaDB (the recommended default);
   `jamal up` starts one locally. Auth requires a database connection.

2. Set the connection variables, a signing secret, and the admin allowlist.
   Better Auth requires a secret of at least 32 bytes. Copy `.env.auth.example`
   to `.env` and fill it in: every `jsails` command loads `.env` (then
   `.env.local` and `.env.<NODE_ENV>`), and the `auth:migrate` / `user:create`
   npm scripts load `.env` and `.env.local` through Node's
   `--env-file-if-exists`. A real environment variable always wins over the
   files, so exporting them in your shell or process manager works too:

   ```sh
   DATABASE_HOST=127.0.0.1
   DATABASE_PORT=3306       # default, optional
   DATABASE_USER=...
   DATABASE_PASSWORD=...
   DATABASE_NAME=...
   BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=...   # openssl rand -base64 32
   ADMIN_EMAILS=you@example.com
   ```

3. Build the server, then run the Better Auth migrations (they run against the
   compiled output, so build first):

   ```sh
   npm run build
   npm run auth:migrate
   ```

4. Create the first account. `name` is optional; email and password are
   required:

   ```sh
   npm run user:create -- you@example.com 'a long password' "Your Name"
   ```

5. Run the app:

   ```sh
   npm run dev
   ```

Visit `/login` to sign in and `/dashboard` to see the signed-in state.

## How it fits together

- Better Auth owns its tables in a MariaDB database you point it at through the
  `DATABASE_*` variables (`DATABASE_HOST`, `DATABASE_PORT`, `DATABASE_USER`,
  `DATABASE_PASSWORD`, `DATABASE_NAME`), a separate schema from any application
  data you add later. `BETTER_AUTH_SECRET` signs its sessions. Keep the
  credentials out of version control.
- `/api/login` and `/api/logout` are plain form targets: the pages post to them
  and the handlers call Better Auth server-side. There is no browser SDK and no
  secret in the client bundle. They are mounted as trusted extension hook routes
  (see `jsails.app.js`), not filesystem API routes, so they bypass the
  filesystem default-deny `authorize` and the session CSRF middleware — a plain
  form POST works even while a session cookie is present.
- Each of those handlers owns its own same-origin check: the request's `Origin`
  must match the configured `publicOrigin` (falling back to the request URL
  origin when unset), and a mismatch is rejected with `403` before any
  credentials are read.
- `POST /api/login` is throttled by the `auth` plugin's default limiter
  (`createMemoryRateLimiter`): 10 attempts per route, email, and client address
  and 100 per client address across all emails in a 15-minute window, answered
  with a generic `429`. Keying on the address too means a stranger cannot lock
  your account out, and the per-address bucket stops one client spraying a
  password over many accounts. Registration and password reset get the same
  limiter, with separate counters, once enabled. Its counters live in process
  memory: with more than one app process, pass a shared (Valkey-backed) hook as
  `login.rateLimit`, `registration.rateLimit`, and `passwordReset.rateLimit`.
  Behind a reverse proxy, also pass `clientAddress` so the limiter sees the
  client rather than the proxy.
- The admin panel at `/admin` admits only a signed-in account whose email is in
  the comma-separated `ADMIN_EMAILS` variable. Unset, nobody is an admin. The
  check matches the email alone, and registration does not verify an address
  before sign-in: before you enable `registration.enabled`, create every
  `ADMIN_EMAILS` account with `npm run user:create` (or require verification),
  or whoever registers a listed address first becomes an admin. Better still,
  switch the panel's `authorize` to `adminOnly` from `@jsails/auth` and grant
  the role with `setUserRole`; sessions already carry `data.role`, and no
  sign-up can set it.
- In production set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the public HTTPS origin.
  `jamal.config.js` forwards it as both `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` and `BETTER_AUTH_URL`;
  Better Auth only marks its session cookie `Secure` when that URL is https.
- `/api/me` returns the current user and is the one protected filesystem API:
  the app's global `authorize` is default-deny, so every filesystem API request
  is rejected unless a session is present. Pages are not covered by `authorize`,
  so `/dashboard` renders a signed-out prompt when the session is absent.
- Login/logout own their origin checks, but they are trusted hook routes and do
  not set a precedent: any authenticated filesystem mutation you add
  (`POST`/`PUT`/`PATCH`/`DELETE` under `api/`) still runs the framework CSRF
  pipeline, which requires a same-origin `Origin` and a matching `X-CSRF-Token`
  for cookie-authenticated requests. Supply `context.session.csrfToken` as that
  header.

## CLI sign-in with `jsails login` (browser-approved)

The same repository builds the web app **and** a small CLI: `commands/*` modules
compile into `dist/commands/` and are run with `jsails <name>`. Three commands
ship:

```sh
jsails login      # request a device code and approve it in the browser
jsails whoami     # print the signed-in account (name and email)
jsails logout     # delete the stored CLI session credentials
```

`jsails login` runs the OAuth 2.0 Device Authorization Grant (RFC 8628) against
the app's Better Auth device endpoints. It requests a code, prints a short user
code and a verification URL, opens the browser for you (pass `--no-open` to
skip), and polls until you approve. Approve it on the `/device` page — the same
page a browser user reaches after choosing "Sign in from a device" on `/login` —
by confirming the user code while signed in. The CLI's base URL comes from
`APP_URL`, then `BETTER_AUTH_URL`, then `http://localhost:3000`.

The resulting access token is a Better Auth **session token** (not a separate
credential), stored on disk so later `jsails whoami` / `jsails logout` runs can
use it:

- **Location:** `${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/jsails/<appName>/credentials.json`,
  where `<appName>` is the package name (or the directory basename when no
  `package.json` is present). The directory is created `0700` and the file is
  written atomically (temp file + rename) with mode `0600`, so other local
  users cannot read the token.
- **Shape:** `{ baseUrl, token, tokenType, expiresAt, scope }`. The token is
  never printed by the CLI.

### Security notes

- The device flow is Better Auth's official RFC 8628 implementation. The
  `client_id` (`jsails-cli`) must match the server's `validateClient` allowlist,
  and approval requires an authenticated session on `/device` — the CLI never
  sees the user's password.
- Tokens are session tokens: revoke one by clearing/rotating its session
  server-side (Better Auth session revocation), and remove the CLI-side copy
  with `jsails logout`. Re-running `jsails login` simply overwrites the
  credentials file.
- The token is stored in plain text on disk (mode `0600`). Treat the account the
  same as a saved browser session, and run `jsails logout` on shared machines.

## Deliberately deferred

- Registration UI — new accounts are created with `npm run user:create`.
- Password reset and email verification.
- OAuth / social sign-in.
- Roles / RBAC — the starter gates `/admin` on the `ADMIN_EMAILS` allowlist;
  `@jsails/auth` ships `adminOnly`, `requireRole`, and `setUserRole` for when
  you move to roles.
- Production hardening — a shared multi-process rate limiter, account lockout,
  and audit logging are not wired.
